                <pb facs="1019" n="925" sameAs="frus:frus1887/pg925"/>

                <head>No. 560.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Neill to Mr. Bayard.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Lima</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1886-12-20"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">December</hi> 20, 1886</date>. (Received
                        January 18, 1887.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 188.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to report to the
                    Department of State the death of José Sevilla, esquire, on the 9th of the
                    present month, at Lima, Peru. He was a naturalized citizen of the United States
                    at the time of his death, and was about eighty years of age. He was a gentleman
                    of large wealth, and well known in Peru as a great philanthropist. He was born
                    at San Pedro de Lloc, in the province of Pacasmayo.</p>
                <p>Mr. Sevilla’s estate is estimated at between $4,000,000 and $5,000,000. He
                    bequeaths the sum of 500,000 silver soles for the establishment of a benevolent
                    institution in New York City, to be called the Sevilla Home for Girls.” I
                    inclose a translation of several of the clauses of the will taken from “El
                    Comereio.”</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Richard R. Neill.</hi></signed></closer>



                <xi:include href="d560-a1.xml" xpointer="d560-a1"/>
            </div>
